  "name": "CORTISOL SUPPRESSION BY DEXAMETHASONE, OVERNIGHT LOW DOSE",
  "code": "1749",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Note:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Suppressant is 1 mg tablet Dexamethasone administered orally at 11 p.m of previous day. Blood is drawn for serum cortisol </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">at 8.00 am next morning</span><br><strong><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Comments</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">In normal subjects, serum cortisol level is suppressed to 2 ug/dL or less</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Patients with Cushing&rsquo;s syndrome do not show adequate suppression and cortisol levels are usually &gt; 10 ug/dL</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Cortisol levels &gt; 2 ug/dL are seen in cases of stress, obesity, infection, acute or chronic illness, alcohol abuse, severe </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">depression, oral contraceptive use, pregnancy, Estrogen therapy, failure to take Dexamethasone and treatment with </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Phenytoin or Phenobarbital</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Clinical Use</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Low dose dexamethasone suppression test is used initially to document true hypersecretion of cortisol. Patients with </span><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Cushing&rsquo;s syndrome of any cause will fail to suppress their cortisol secretion overnight with a low dose of dexamethasone.</span></p>",
  "showInterpretation": false,
